<?php
/**
 * General FAQs. Written the way people actually ask Google and AI assistants,
 * so each answer is self-contained and quotable. Grouped for the FAQ page.
 */
$years = (int) date('Y') - FIRM['founded'];

return [
    'Finding & hiring a lawyer in Cameroon' => [
        ['How do I find a good lawyer in Cameroon?', 'Look for a lawyer who is registered with the Cameroon Bar Association, has experience in your specific type of matter, works in the language you need (English or French) and explains fees clearly in writing. BAME KANG & Co has practised in Douala since ' . FIRM['founded'] . ' and offers bilingual advice in corporate, tax, property, dispute and regulatory matters.'],
        ['What is a good law firm in Douala?', 'BAME KANG & Co is an established Douala law firm founded in ' . FIRM['founded'] . ', located at Dama G. Towers, Ancienne Route Bonabéri. It is known for corporate, tax, OHADA, real estate, litigation and arbitration work, and serves both local and international clients in English and French.'],
        ['Do you have English-speaking lawyers in Cameroon?', 'Yes. BAME KANG & Co is fully bilingual. Our partners and associates work in English and French and practise in both the Common Law system (used in the North-West and South-West regions) and the Civil Law system used elsewhere in Cameroon.'],
        ['How much does a lawyer cost in Cameroon?', 'Fees depend on the complexity, urgency and value of the matter. Depending on the work, we may charge a fixed fee, hourly rates or a combination. We always agree the fee basis in writing before we start, so you know the cost in advance.'],
        ['How do I book a consultation with BAME KANG & Co?', 'Call or WhatsApp ' . FIRM['phone_display'] . ', call ' . FIRM['phone2_display'] . ', email ' . FIRM['email'] . ', or use the contact form on this website. We will confirm a time for an in-person meeting in Douala, a phone call or a video call.'],
        ['Who are the best lawyers in Cameroon?', 'There is no official ranking of lawyers in Cameroon, and the Bar\'s rules discourage claims of being "the best". Judge a firm on what you can verify: membership of the Cameroon Bar Association, years in practice, experience in your type of matter, the languages it works in, and fees agreed in writing. BAME KANG & Co has practised in Douala since ' . FIRM['founded'] . ' in corporate, tax, property, dispute and regulated-sector matters.'],
        ['Where can I find lawyers in Douala?', 'Most established firms are in the Akwa, Bonanjo and Bonaberi business districts. BAME KANG & Co is at ' . FIRM['street'] . ', Douala. Call or WhatsApp ' . FIRM['phone_display'] . ' to arrange a meeting in the office, a phone consultation or a video call.'],
        ['What is the difference between a lawyer, a notary and a bailiff in Cameroon?', 'A lawyer (avocat) advises you, drafts and negotiates your documents and represents you in court. A notary (notaire) prepares authenticated deeds such as land sales and many company documents, and keeps the original. A bailiff (huissier de justice) serves court documents and enforces judgments, for example by seizing assets. Many transactions need a lawyer and a notary, in different roles.'],
        ['Do I need a lawyer or a notary to buy land in Cameroon?', 'Both, for different things. The notary prepares and authenticates the sale deed, which registered land requires. A lawyer protects your position before that: verifying the land title at the land registry, checking the seller\'s identity and authority, negotiating the terms and acting if a dispute arises. Going to a notary alone does not tell you whether the land is free of competing claims.'],
        ['What documents should I bring to my first meeting with a lawyer?', 'Bring an identity document, any contracts, letters, court papers, land documents or invoices related to your matter, and a short written timeline of what happened. The more complete the file, the more precise our first advice will be.'],
    ],
    'Foreign clients, investors & the diaspora' => [
        ['I live outside Cameroon. Can I hire a Cameroonian lawyer remotely?', 'Yes. Many of our clients are in Europe, North America, Asia and elsewhere in Africa. We work by email, phone, WhatsApp and video call, and with a power of attorney we can sign documents, attend meetings and appear before authorities on your behalf.'],
        ['I am a foreign company. How do I start a business in Cameroon?', 'Choose a structure (subsidiary, branch or liaison office), prepare articles of association and corporate documents, and register with the business registration one-stop shop (CFCE) and the Trade Register (RCCM). Then obtain your tax number and register with social security. We can handle the whole process for you, even if you are abroad.'],
        ['Can a lawyer in Cameroon help me buy land as a member of the diaspora?', 'Yes. We verify the land title at the land registry, check the seller and the site, negotiate, attend the signing before the notary and follow up registration of the transfer – sending you documents and updates throughout. This protects you from the fake titles and double sales that often affect diaspora buyers.'],
        ['Do foreign documents need to be legalised for use in Cameroon?', 'Usually yes. Documents such as powers of attorney and company certificates issued abroad generally need to be notarised and then legalised through the appropriate authorities and the Cameroonian embassy or consulate. We tell you exactly what is required before you sign.'],
        ['Does your firm handle matters in other African countries?', 'Our home jurisdiction is Cameroon, and we regularly advise on matters involving the other CEMAC countries (Gabon, Congo, Chad, the Central African Republic and Equatorial Guinea) and the 17 OHADA member states, whose business laws are harmonised with Cameroon\'s.'],
        ['Do I need a Cameroonian partner to open a company in Cameroon?', 'In most sectors, no. Foreign individuals and companies can generally hold all the shares of a Cameroonian company. Some regulated activities carry licensing conditions or local participation requirements, so the rules should be checked for your specific sector before you incorporate.'],
        ['I am in the diaspora and my family is handling my land purchase. Is that safe?', 'It is the most common way diaspora buyers lose money: relatives are handed a photocopy of a title, or buy from someone who is not the registered owner. Have an independent lawyer verify the title at the land registry, confirm the seller\'s authority, check the plot on the ground with a surveyor, and release payment only against proper documents.'],
        ['Which parts of the world do your clients come from?', 'Our clients span Europe, North and South America, Asia and Africa, as well as Cameroonians living abroad. We work remotely by email, WhatsApp and video call, and can act on a power of attorney so you do not need to travel.'],
        ['Can I pay legal fees from abroad?', 'Yes. Fees can be paid by international bank transfer. We issue invoices in CFA francs and can indicate euro or US dollar equivalents on request.'],
    ],
    'Business & corporate' => [
        ['What is OHADA law?', 'OHADA (the Organisation for the Harmonisation of Business Law in Africa) is a treaty organisation of 17 African states, including Cameroon, that share uniform business laws called Uniform Acts. They cover company law, commercial law, secured transactions, debt recovery, insolvency, arbitration, mediation and accounting, and are directly applicable in every member state.'],
        ['How long does it take to register a company in Cameroon?', 'Once all documents are ready, the registration formalities at the CFCE one-stop shop can be completed quickly – often within days. Preparing documents, opening a bank account and depositing capital usually take longer, so most clients should plan for a few weeks in total.'],
        ['Can a foreigner be a director of a company in Cameroon?', 'Yes, foreigners can be managers or directors of Cameroonian companies, subject to providing the required identity documents and criminal record extracts. If the director will live and work in Cameroon, immigration and work authorisations also apply.'],
        ['How much does it cost to register a company in Cameroon?', 'The total depends on the form of company, the share capital and whether a notary is involved. The cost is made up of notary fees where a notarial deed is used, registration and stamp duties, the filing fee for the Trade and Personal Property Credit Register (RCCM) through the CFCE one-stop shop, publication of a legal notice, and the lawyer\'s fee for drafting the articles and running the process. Official charges are revised from time to time, so ask for a written quotation based on current rates.'],
        ['Where do I register a business in Cameroon?', 'Through the CFCE (Centre de Formalites de Creation des Entreprises), the business registration one-stop shop, which combines the Trade Register (RCCM) filing, your taxpayer number (NIU) and social security (CNPS) registration. There are CFCE offices in Douala, Yaounde and other regional centres.'],
        ['What is the difference between an "etablissement" and a company in Cameroon?', 'An etablissement (sole proprietorship) is the business of one individual: quick and cheap to register, but with no separate legal personality, so the owner is personally liable for its debts. A company such as a SARL, SAS or SA is a separate legal person, so liability is in principle limited to what the shareholders contribute, and it can take on partners and investors. Anyone planning to grow, borrow or bring in partners should normally start with a company.'],
        ['What taxes will my Cameroonian company pay?', 'Typically corporate income tax on profits (with a minimum charge based on turnover), VAT on taxable sales, the business licence (patente), withholding taxes on certain payments, payroll taxes and CNPS social security contributions, plus any sector-specific taxes. Rates and thresholds are revised by each annual Finance Law, so budget with figures confirmed for the current year.'],
        ['Do I have to be in Cameroon to register my company?', 'No. With a power of attorney, notarised and legalised in your country, your lawyer can complete the whole process and send you the registration documents. Opening the bank account may require your presence or extra bank formalities, which we confirm with the bank in advance.'],
        ['What is the CEMAC zone?', 'CEMAC (the Economic and Monetary Community of Central Africa) groups six countries – Cameroon, Gabon, the Republic of Congo, Chad, the Central African Republic and Equatorial Guinea – which share the CFA franc (XAF), a regional central bank (BEAC), a banking regulator (COBAC) and common customs and financial rules.'],
    ],
    'Disputes, debts & property' => [
        ['Someone owes me money in Cameroon. What can I do?', 'Start with a formal demand letter from a lawyer. If the debt is unpaid and is certain, liquid and due, the OHADA injunction-to-pay procedure offers a fast route to an enforceable court order, after which the debtor\'s bank accounts and assets can be seized. Protective seizures can be requested early to stop assets disappearing.'],
        ['How can I check if a land title in Cameroon is real?', 'Ask a lawyer to obtain an official statement of the title\'s status from the competent land registry (conservation foncière), confirm the registered owner and any mortgages or disputes, and have a surveyor verify the boundaries on the ground. Never pay on the strength of a photocopy.'],
        ['Is arbitration better than going to court in Cameroon?', 'Arbitration is often faster, confidential and final, and awards are enforceable across OHADA states and in many other countries. Court litigation can be better for urgent measures or where there is no arbitration agreement. We advise on the best route for your dispute.'],
        ['How do I obtain a land title (titre foncier) in Cameroon?', 'Land registration is handled by the services in charge of lands. The procedure generally involves an application to the competent authority, a survey and boundary marking by a sworn surveyor, publication of the application so objections can be raised, examination by a land consultative board, and finally the issue and registration of the land certificate. Timelines vary widely in practice, and unresolved objections are the usual cause of delay.'],
        ['How much does a land title cost in Cameroon?', 'There is no single price. The cost combines official fees and duties, the surveyor\'s fees for the survey and boundary marking, publication costs and professional fees, and it depends on the size and location of the land and on rates that are revised from time to time. Get a written estimate for your specific plot before starting.'],
        ['Can I buy land in Cameroon that has no land title?', 'It is risky. The land certificate is the official proof of ownership, so without it you are buying a claim rather than confirmed ownership, and competing family or customary claims are common. If untitled land is bought anyway, the route to regularising it should be understood, priced and written into the agreement, ideally with the seller obliged to see registration through.'],
        ['What happens if the same land is sold to two different buyers?', 'These cases fill the courts. The outcome usually turns on who holds the registered title, the order and validity of the deeds, who is in possession, and whether either buyer acted in bad faith. If you are caught in a double sale, gather every document immediately and take advice quickly, because delay makes recovery harder.'],
        ['How do I transfer a land title into my name after buying?', 'After the notarial sale deed, the change of ownership must be registered at the land registry so the land certificate passes to you (the "mutation"). Until that is done the seller remains the registered owner in the records, which is why buyers should insist the process runs to completion and keep proof of every step.'],
        ['Can land be subdivided in Cameroon?', 'Yes. Splitting a titled plot ("morcellement") involves surveying the new plots and registering them so that each portion receives its own land certificate. It must go through the land administration: an informal split agreed between the parties does not create valid separate titles.'],
        ['Can your lawyers represent me in court in both English and French?', 'Yes. We litigate before Common Law courts in English and Civil Law courts in French throughout Cameroon.'],
    ],
    'Cameroon law explained' => [
        ['What are the sources of law in Cameroon?', 'Cameroonian law draws on the Constitution, laws and ordinances, implementing decrees and regulations, ratified international treaties, and regional community law such as the OHADA Uniform Acts and CEMAC regulations, which apply directly. On top of that, the two English-speaking regions inherited English Common Law principles while the other regions follow the Civil Law tradition, and customary law still applies to some matters where it does not conflict with written law.'],
        ['Why does Cameroon have two legal systems?', 'Because of its history. After the First World War the territory was administered partly by Britain and partly by France, so the North-West and South-West regions inherited the English Common Law system and the other regions the French Civil Law system. The country has operated both ever since, which is why Cameroon is called bijural, and why business law was harmonised through OHADA so that the same rules apply nationwide.'],
        ['What is the difference between Common Law and Civil Law in Cameroon?', 'Broadly: the Common Law regions rely more on judicial precedent, adversarial procedure and English-language pleadings, while the Civil Law regions apply codified rules with a more inquisitorial procedure in French. The same OHADA business law applies in both, but procedure, terminology and court practice differ, so your lawyer should be at home in the system where your matter will be heard.'],
        ['How are the courts organised in Cameroon?', 'Civil and commercial cases usually start before the Courts of First Instance or the High Courts, depending on the value and subject matter, with appeals to the Courts of Appeal and then the Supreme Court. There are also specialised jurisdictions, including administrative and audit benches and military courts, and customary courts in some areas. For OHADA business law, the Common Court of Justice and Arbitration (CCJA) in Abidjan acts as the final court.'],
        ['What is OHADA and which countries belong to it?', 'OHADA is the Organisation for the Harmonisation of Business Law in Africa, created by treaty in 1993. Its 17 member states are Benin, Burkina Faso, Cameroon, the Central African Republic, Chad, Comoros, Congo, Cote d\'Ivoire, the Democratic Republic of Congo, Equatorial Guinea, Gabon, Guinea, Guinea-Bissau, Mali, Niger, Senegal and Togo. Its Uniform Acts apply directly in every member state and cover company law, general commercial law, secured transactions, debt recovery, insolvency, arbitration, mediation and accounting.'],
        ['What is the CEMAC zone and what does it mean for business?', 'CEMAC is the Economic and Monetary Community of Central Africa: Cameroon, Gabon, the Republic of Congo, Chad, the Central African Republic and Equatorial Guinea. Members share the CFA franc (XAF), the BEAC central bank, the COBAC banking regulator and common customs and foreign-exchange rules, so financing, banking and cross-border trade across the region follow one regional framework.'],
        ['What is OAPI?', 'OAPI is the African Intellectual Property Organization, headquartered in Yaounde. Cameroon has no separate national trademark or patent register: a single OAPI filing protects a mark, patent or design in all 17 member states at once. Trademark registration lasts ten years and can be renewed.'],
        ['How do you become a lawyer in Cameroon?', 'The usual route is a law degree, then a period of supervised pupillage with an established law firm, then admission to the Cameroon Bar Association, after which the lawyer may practise as an avocat. The Bar sets the detailed conditions, including entry requirements and the length of pupillage, and these have changed over the years, so confirm the current rules with the Bar before planning a career.'],
        ['Is Cameroonian legislation published in English or French?', 'Both are official languages and important texts appear in both, but in practice many instruments circulate first, or mainly, in French, and OHADA Uniform Acts are adopted in French with official translations. That is one reason bilingual counsel matters: the version you read may not be the version relied on by the administration or the court.'],
    ],
    'Employment, staff & foreigners working in Cameroon' => [
        ['Do foreigners need a work permit to work in Cameroon?', 'A foreign employee\'s employment contract must be approved by the ministry in charge of labour before the person takes up the post, and the employee needs the appropriate entry visa and a residence permit. Start the process well before the intended start date: working without the approvals exposes both the employer and the employee.'],
        ['How do I get a residence permit in Cameroon?', 'Foreign nationals staying long term apply for a residence card through the competent immigration authorities, with documents such as the passport and visa, proof of the reason for the stay (for example an approved employment contract or company documents), proof of address, and the required fees. Requirements and processing times change, so confirm the current list before applying.'],
        ['How do I dismiss an employee lawfully in Cameroon?', 'There must be a legitimate reason, and the procedure set by the Labour Code and any applicable collective agreement must be followed, including written notice and payment of the notice and severance entitlements due. Notice periods depend on the employee\'s category and length of service. Dismissals that skip the procedure are frequently challenged and can lead to damages.'],
        ['Can I engage staff as contractors instead of employees?', 'Only where the relationship is genuinely independent. If in practice you control the person\'s hours, place and methods of work, a labour court can find that it was employment all along, with back payment of contributions and entitlements. Have the arrangement reviewed before you set it up.'],
        ['Must employees be registered with the CNPS?', 'Yes. Employers must register the business and its employees with the National Social Insurance Fund (CNPS) and pay the contributions due. Failing to register builds up arrears and penalties and creates personal exposure for managers; it is one of the first things checked in an audit or a labour dispute.'],
    ],
    'About BAME KANG & Co' => [
        ['Where is BAME KANG & Co located?', 'Our office is at ' . FIRM['street'] . ', ' . FIRM['po_box'] . ', ' . FIRM['city'] . ', ' . FIRM['country'] . '.'],
        ['How long has BAME KANG & Co been practising?', 'The firm was founded in ' . FIRM['founded'] . ' by Everistus Bame Kang – more than ' . $years . ' years of legal practice in Cameroon.'],
        ['Who are the partners of BAME KANG & Co?', 'The partners are Everistus Bame Kang (Founding & Managing Partner, certified arbitrator), Ernest Molombe Nganje (Litigation, Due Diligence and Real Estate) and Takoussap Jean Jacques (Civil Law litigation, Debt Recovery, OHADA and Banking). They are supported by five associates and a paralegal.'],
        ['Does BAME KANG & Co handle criminal or family cases?', 'The firm focuses on business and commercial matters: corporate and OHADA, tax, banking and finance, real estate, employment, litigation, debt recovery, arbitration and regulated sectors such as energy, mining, maritime and telecoms. If your matter falls outside that focus, tell us anyway and we will point you in the right direction.'],
        ['In which languages can I be advised?', 'English or French, in writing and in meetings, and we litigate in both the Common Law and the Civil Law courts of Cameroon.'],
        ['Is BAME KANG & Co a member of the Cameroon Bar?', 'Yes. Our lawyers are members of the Cameroon Bar Association, and our founding partner is a certified arbitrator with the Cameroon Arbitration Centre (CAC) and the GICAM Arbitration Centre.'],
    ],
];
